-- title:
--   The grouped polynomial of rectangular sums
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Let $f$ be a function on Boolean matrices with rectangular sums $C_{k,l}(f)$.
--
--   1. **Grouped polynomial.**
--   $$
--   G_f(a,b,c)=\sum_{k\le|\iota|}\sum_{l\le|\kappa|}C_{k,l}(f)\,a^kb^lc^{kl} .
--   $$
--   For rank-one supported $f$ this equals the damped expectation with biases $a,b,c$. The exponent $kl$ of $c$ records the size of the rectangle.
